### What is System.out?

In Java, every console message passes through `System.out`:
- `System`: A built-in class in `java.lang` that provides access to system resources.
- `out`: A static member of `System` representing the **Standard Output Stream** (an instance of `PrintStream`).
- `println()` / `print()`: Methods on the `out` object that send text to your terminal screen.

---

### print() vs println() — The Cursor Rule

The difference between `print()` and `println()` comes down to where the terminal cursor stops after outputting text:

1. **`System.out.print(...)`**:
   - Prints the text.
   - Leaves the cursor immediately at the end of the text on the **same line**.
   - The next print statement will start right where this one finished.

2. **`System.out.println(...)`**:
   - Prints the text.
   - Moves the cursor to the **beginning of the next line** (appends an invisible line-feed `\n`).
   - Calling `System.out.println();` with no arguments simply prints a blank empty line.

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // print leaves cursor on the same line:
        System.out.print("Hello ");
        System.out.print("World!");
        // Output on Line 1: Hello World!

        System.out.println(); // moves cursor to Line 2

        // println advances cursor to next line after printing:
        System.out.println("First Line");
        System.out.println("Second Line");
    }
}
```

---

### Combining Text and Variables with `+`

The `+` operator in Java is overloaded:
- When both operands are numbers: it performs mathematical addition (`10 + 5 = 15`).
- When at least one operand is a `String`: it performs **String concatenation** (joins them into a single string).

```java
int score = 95;
String player = "Rahul";

// Concatenation:
System.out.println("Player " + player + " scored " + score + " points.");
```

> [!CAUTION]
> **Operator Precedence in Concatenation Trap**:
> Java evaluates `+` from left to right.
> - `System.out.println("Total: " + 10 + 20);` prints `Total: 1020` (because `"Total: " + 10` becomes `"Total: 10"`, which is then concatenated with `20`).
> - Use parentheses to force math first: `System.out.println("Total: " + (10 + 20));` prints `Total: 30`.

---

### Escape Sequences

When you need special characters inside a string literal, prefix them with a backslash `\`:

| Escape Sequence | Name | Effect |
|---|---|---|
| `\n` | Newline | Moves the cursor down to the next line |
| `\t` | Tab | Inserts horizontal whitespace alignment (typically 4 or 8 spaces) |
| `\"` | Double Quote | Allows printing a literal quote character inside string quotes |
| `\\` | Backslash | Prints a literal backslash character |

```java
System.out.println("Item\tQty\tPrice");
System.out.println("Pen\t3\tRs 30");
System.out.println("Book\t1\tRs 150");
System.out.println("He said, \"Learn Java daily!\"");
```

